<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vincenzo Scamozzi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1548–1616</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vincenzo Scamozzi was an Italian architect from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, the leading successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-palladio">Andrea Palladio</a>. He completed Palladio's Teatro Olimpico in Vicenza, adding its famous perspective street scenery, designed the Procuratie Nuove on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">Saint Mark</a>'s Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teatro-allantica">Teatro all'Antica</a> at Sabbioneta. His treatise L'idea della architettura universale (1615) influenced architects across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including Inigo Jones.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vincenzo-scamozzi.jpg" alt="Vincenzo Scamozzi" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Veronese · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scamozzi_portrait_by_Veronese.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
